olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Britain=E2=80=99s national debt is rising =
faster than expected after the government borrowed more than expected, agai=
n, to balance the books.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The latest public finances data, just rele=
ased, shows that the UK borrowed =C2=A318.3bn in August, which is =C2=A32.9=
bn more than in August 2025, as spending rose faster than government income=
.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This is higher than the =C2=A315.6bn forec=
ast by the City. But more importantly, it=E2=80=99s =C2=A33.5bn above the O=
ffice for Budget Responsibility (OBR)=E2=80=98s forecast for August.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">And it means that so far this financial ye=
ar, the UK has borrowed =C2=A38.1bn more than the OBR=E2=80=99s forecast.</=
p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This all adds up to a headache for chancel=
lor John Healey as he works on next month=E2=80=99s budget, as the recent b=
ond market turmoil has eaten into his =E2=80=98headroom=E2=80=99 to keep wi=
thin the fiscal rules.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">ONS senior statistician Tom Davies said: =
=E2=80=9CBorrowing in the financial year so far was lower than over the sam=
e period last year. However, it was higher than the official forecast, larg=
ely because central government borrowed more than anticipated.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9COn the month, borrowing was up by=
 almost a fifth on last August, as spending increased more than government =
income partly reflecting the impacts of inflation.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Emeritus professor Joe Nellis, head of eco=
nomic research at accountancy and advisory firm MHA, says this morning=E2=
=80=99s data is =E2=80=9Canother reminder of the fiscal straitjacket facing=
 the government=E2=80=9D before the budget.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Nellis adds: =E2=80=9CBut why is the defic=
it proving so hard to reduce? The weakness lies mainly on the expenditure s=
ide. Higher inflation is impacting spending on public-sector pay, state ben=
efits and pensions. And last week=E2=80=99s announcement that inflation has=
 hit 3.1% will not have helped.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9COn top of this, the cost of servi=
cing the national debt remains exceptionally high. Public sector net debt i=
s just below =C2=A33tn, representing about 94% of GDP, the highest since th=
e early 1960s.=E2=80=9D<strong><br><br></strong></p>
